&lt;/div&gt;`







html`
&lt;div class="dash-notes"&gt;
  &lt;div class="dash-notes-title"&gt;Cross-cutting notes for this problem&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;ul class="dash-notes-list"&gt;
    ${notes.map(n =&gt; html`&lt;li&gt;${boldify(n)}&lt;/li&gt;`)}
  &lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;`







// Render a string with **bold** markup as an HTML fragment — pure JS, no imports,
// no network (C8). Splits on the **…** delimiters and wraps the odd segments in &lt;b&gt;.
boldify = (s) =&gt; {
  const parts = s.split("**");
  return html`&lt;span&gt;${parts.map((seg, i) =&gt; i % 2 === 1 ? html`&lt;b&gt;${seg}&lt;/b&gt;` : seg)}&lt;/span&gt;`;
}

3. Code idiom
The course idiom is from scratch: notebook 17 extends the autodiff Value engine (adding tanh and exp) and then defines Neuron / Layer / MLP as pure-Python classes — no NumPy tensors, scalar values only, so every gradient is visible. The canonical build is MLP(3, [3, 3, 1]): 3 inputs, two hidden layers of 3 tanh neurons, and a single tanh output.
The model classes (notebook 17_Neural_nets_basics.ipynb, cell-10). A Neuron is a weighted sum plus bias, squashed by tanh; a Layer is a list of neurons; an MLP chains layers:
import random

class Neuron:
    def __init__(self, nin):
        self.w = [Value(random.uniform(-1, 1)) for _ in range(nin)]  # one weight per input
        self.b = Value(random.uniform(-1, 1))                        # ← NO seed set: non-deterministic
    def __call__(self, x):
        act = sum((wi*xi for wi, xi in zip(self.w, x)), self.b)      # Σ w_kj x_j + bias
        return act.tanh()                                           # activation g(·) = tanh
    def parameters(self):
        return self.w + [self.b]

class Layer:
    def __init__(self, nin, nout):
        self.neurons = [Neuron(nin) for _ in range(nout)]
    def __call__(self, x):
        outs = [n(x) for n in self.neurons]
        return outs[0] if len(outs) == 1 else outs
    def parameters(self):
        return [p for neuron in self.neurons for p in neuron.parameters()]

class MLP:
    def __init__(self, nin, nouts):
        sz = [nin] + nouts                                          # [3, 3, 3, 1]
        self.layers = [Layer(sz[i], sz[i+1]) for i in range(len(nouts))]
    def __call__(self, x):
        for layer in self.layers:
            x = layer(x)
        return x
    def parameters(self):
        return [p for layer in self.layers for p in layer.parameters()]
The training loop (notebook 17_Neural_nets_basics.ipynb, cell-14). Full-batch gradient descent on a sum-of-squared-errors loss (no averaging), learning rate 1.0, 200 iterations — all hard-coded:
n = MLP(3, [3, 3, 1])                                  # the default architecture
for k in range(200):                                   # n_iter = 200  (hard-coded)
    ypred = [n(x) for x in xs]
    loss = sum((yout - ygt)**2 for ygt, yout in zip(ys, ypred))  # SSE — summed, not averaged

    for p in n.parameters():                           # zero the gradients
        p.grad = 0.0
    loss.backward()                                    # backprop through the whole graph

    for p in n.parameters():                           # update step
        p.data += -1.0 * p.grad                         # lr = 1.0  (the −1.0 multiplier)
The three “hyperparameters” worth marking: the architecture [3, 3, 1] (the default in this notebook), the activation tanh (every neuron, including the output), the learning rate 1.0, and n_iter = 200. A learning rate of 1.0 is unusually large — it works only because the dataset is a 4-sample toy (see the spike in §4).

Idiom note — no seed. Neuron initializes weights with random.uniform(-1, 1) and the notebook never calls random.seed(...), so each run starts from different weights and prints different per-iteration losses (phase0/notebook_17_NN_basics.md). The worked example below fixes a seed purely to make the numbers reproducible; the notebook as written is non-deterministic.

5. Visualization
The one picture that earns its place is the activation functions — the nonlinearity is the entire reason a stack of layers is more than a single linear map. Sigmoid and tanh both saturate (flatten) for large |z|, which is exactly where their gradient g'(z) goes to zero and learning stalls (the vanishing-gradient problem, §6); ReLU stays linear for z &gt; 0, which is why modern nets default to it.

famNeural = (getComputedStyle(document.documentElement).getPropertyValue('--family-neural').trim() || "#7B2CBF")
actData = {
  const zs = d3.range(-6, 6.01, 0.05);
  const sigmoid = z =&gt; 1/(1+Math.exp(-z));
  const tanh = z =&gt; Math.tanh(z);
  const relu = z =&gt; Math.max(0, z);
  const rows = [];
  for (const z of zs) {
    rows.push({z, g: sigmoid(z), fn: "sigmoid"});
    rows.push({z, g: tanh(z),    fn: "tanh"});
    rows.push({z, g: relu(z),    fn: "ReLU"});
  }
  return rows;
}
Plot.plot({
  height: 320, marginLeft: 50, marginRight: 70, grid: true,
  x: {label: "z (pre-activation)"},
  y: {label: "g(z)", domain: [-1.2, 3]},
  color: {legend: true, domain: ["sigmoid", "tanh", "ReLU"],
          range: [famNeural, "#C77DFF", "#3D405B"]},
  marks: [
    Plot.ruleY([0], {stroke: "#ccc"}),
    Plot.ruleX([0], {stroke: "#ccc"}),
    Plot.line(actData, {x: "z", y: "g", stroke: "fn", strokeWidth: 2.5})
  ]
})












