Figure 1: kNN classifies the red star (test point) by majority vote of its K=3 nearest neighbors (ringed), on three deterministic 2-D blobs. The dashed segments are the distances kNN ranks; the star is colored by the winning class. Reload to draw a new test point: where it lands among the blobs decides the vote — near a boundary the K=3 ring can straddle two colors, which is exactly where the choice of K matters most.




This is the picture that earns its place for kNN: it makes the algorithm itself visible. The dashed segments are the very distances kNN computes and ranks; the three ringed points are the vote; the star takes their majority color. It also shows why K matters — drop the test point near a blob boundary and the K = 3 ring straddles two colors, the regime where small K is jumpy (high variance) and a larger K would steady the vote. This mirrors the notebook’s own visualization idiom (a starred test point with its nearest neighbors highlighted on make_blobs data, cell 10), rebuilt with deterministic in-browser data so it needs no network or stored dataset.

5. Visualization
The sigmoid is the one picture that earns its place: it shows why a linear z becomes a probability. The figure below plots \sigma(z) = 1/(1+e^{-z}) — flat near 0 and 1, steepest at z=0 where \sigma(0)=0.5 (the decision threshold). Static prose can state “the sigmoid squashes \mathbb{R} into [0,1]”; only the curve shows the S-shape and where the 0.5 cut falls.

famLinear = (getComputedStyle(document.documentElement).getPropertyValue('--family-linear').trim() || "#2E86AB")
sigmoidData = {
  const sig = z =&gt; 1 / (1 + Math.exp(-z));
  return d3.range(-8, 8.01, 0.1).map(z =&gt; ({z, p: sig(z)}));
}
Plot.plot({
  height: 300, marginLeft: 50, grid: true,
  x: {label: "z = β₀ + β₁x₁ + … (net input)", domain: [-8, 8]},
  y: {label: "σ(z) = P(class 1)", domain: [0, 1]},
