5. Visualization
The one picture that earns its place here is the contrast between the two activation functions the lecture puts side by side (Lecture 9, slides 8–9, citing ISL): ReLU, used on this network’s hidden layer, versus the sigmoid it replaced. ReLU is max(0, z) — exactly the z1.clamp(min=0) in the code idiom — a hard hinge that is flat-zero for negatives and linear for positives; sigmoid is the smooth S-curve 1/(1+e^{-z}) that saturates at both ends. The figure makes visible why ReLU dominates modern hidden layers: its gradient is a clean 1 for any positive input (so error signals pass back undiminished), whereas the sigmoid’s gradient flattens toward zero in both tails — the “vanishing gradient” that stalls deep backprop.

